\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces A pre-LN Transformer encoder block. Each sub-layer is wrapped in a residual connection; LayerNorm is applied before the sub-layer (pre-norm).}}{17}{figure.caption.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The four months of real gamma-dose recordings. Each panel is a continuous $\sim 40$k-sample segment at one sample per minute. The signal is centred around $\sim 99$ counts with a high-frequency dispersion of a few counts and slow drifts of a few units; a small number of sharper excursions are visible.}}{20}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces A $10\,000$-sample snapshot of the first month decomposed into a slowly-varying baseline (top, with the MA-480 baseline in red) and zero-mean residuals (bottom).}}{21}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left}: empirical distribution of the raw gamma dose values per month. The distributions are unimodal and only slightly skewed, supporting a Gaussian approximation of the high-frequency noise. \textbf {Right}: residual autocorrelation (after MA-480 baseline removal). The non-trivial decay over $\sim 30$ lags is the v1 noise model's biggest oversight; we will come back to it in \cref {chap:reanalysis}.}}{22}{figure.caption.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The six v1 anomaly templates, drawn in normalised time and amplitude. Each template is encoded as a small list of keypoints (red dots) connected by linear, exponential or half-cosine (``bell'') segments.}}{24}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Five stochastic realisations of each v1 template with the parameter ranges of \cref {eq:v1-ranges}. The qualitative shape is preserved, but no two realisations are identical.}}{25}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces First $30\,000$ samples (of $10^{7}$) of the v1 synthetic dataset. Anomaly spans are coloured by class. Note the dense injection: roughly four visible bumps in a $30$k-sample slice.}}{26}{figure.caption.10}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Sliding-window construction on a fragment of the v2 synthetic signal. Each coloured bar represents one $512$-sample training window; red bars indicate windows that capture $\geq 30\%$ of an underlying event's samples and so inherit its class label (event-coverage rule, \cref {sec:framing}).}}{30}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left}: window-level class distribution at training, for v1 (seven classes, blue) and v2 (four classes, green), against the (estimated) real deployment prior in red ($\sim 99\%$ \texttt {Normal}). v1 has been trained with a prior that is roughly the \emph {opposite} of the deployment prior. v2 closes the gap to within a factor of three. \textbf {Right}: cross-entropy class weights used in each iteration. v2 strongly down-weights \texttt {Normal} ($w_0 = 0.27$ vs $\sim 9$ on the anomaly classes) because the class imbalance in v2 is also much larger than in v1.}}{31}{figure.caption.16}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces v1 training dynamics. Left: train (dashed) and validation (solid) loss for the three models. Right: validation macro F1 (window level). All three models converge within $35$ epochs and benefit from one or two learning-rate reductions.}}{32}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces v1 event-level confusion matrices on the synthetic test segment. Cell colour is the row-normalised recall; the integer inside each cell is the raw event count. The residual network achieves a perfect classification; the attention model misses three events; the baseline 1D-CNN misses four events.}}{33}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces v1 robustness curves. Left: macro F1 vs. background-noise multiplier. Right: macro F1 vs. anomaly-shape deformation multiplier. The dotted line marks $50\%$ F1. The 1D-CNN drops sharply to a $\sim 7\%$ plateau under noise --- the hallmark of a classifier that has lost confidence in its features and collapses to a single class. The residual and attention models degrade much more gracefully.}}{34}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces v1 inference on the first $10\,000$ samples of February 2015. Blue: raw gamma dose (left axis). Coloured lines: per-class confidence smoothed with a $30$-sample rolling mean (right axis, $0$--$100\%$). The grey curve is the \texttt {Normal Background} confidence; the other colours are the six anomaly classes.}}{35}{figure.caption.21}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Diagnostics on the masked residual of February 2015. \textbf {Top-left}: marginal histogram, near-perfectly fit by $\mathcal {N}(0, 2.21)$. \textbf {Top-right}: Q-Q plot vs.\ Normal, on the diagonal. \textbf {Bottom-left}: autocorrelation function on the clean segment --- $\mathrm {ACF}(1) = 0.864$, decaying smoothly to zero by lag $\sim 30$. \textbf {Bottom-right}: rolling standard deviation, stationary around the global value.}}{39}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces AR(1) innovation diagnostics, February 2015. Top-left: histogram + Gaussian fit. Top-right: Q-Q vs.\ Normal. Bottom-left: ACF of innovations, with bars inside or barely outside the $\pm 2 / \sqrt {N}$ band. Bottom-right: Ljung--Box summary.}}{40}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Empirical residual periodogram (grey scatter + blue smoothed line) against the theoretical PSDs of AR(p) fits. AR(1), AR(2) and AR(5) produce essentially indistinguishable theoretical spectra, all of which match the empirical periodogram across five decades of frequency.}}{41}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces Per-quarter AR(1) parameter estimates. \textbf {Left}: $\varphi $, stable around $\sim 0.87$ except for an early-month excursion in October likely caused by a few unmasked outliers. \textbf {Right}: innovation std $\sigma _\varepsilon $, stable around $\sim 1.12$. The dashed lines mark the values used in the v2 generator.}}{41}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces The four months of real 2015 data with hand-labelled event spans (orange). Across the four months there are $3 + 3 + 8 + 3 = 17$ labelled events.}}{43}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.6}{\ignorespaces Zoomed view around each of the eight hand-labelled events in June 2015 ($\pm 600$ samples of context). The orange band marks the labelled interval; coloured lines are MA-30 (red) and MA-120 (green) smoothings. The first four events are very short ($30$--$55$ samples) sharp spikes; the last four are longer ($138$--$281$ samples), with a mix of bell and asymmetric shapes.}}{44}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.7}{\ignorespaces Empirical event statistics from the seventeen hand labels. \textbf {Left}: durations span $[32, 1014]$ samples, with most events below $400$. \textbf {Right}: peak amplitudes (above the MA-4320 local baseline) span $[11.2, 36.8]$ counts. The red dashed lines mark the v2 generator's $[30, 1000]$-sample and $[15, 40]$ count bounds.}}{45}{figure.caption.30}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left}: $600$ samples of IID Gaussian noise ($\sigma = 2.30$, v1) and of AR(1) noise ($\varphi = 0.86$, $\sigma _\varepsilon = 1.12$, v2) sharing the same marginal standard deviation. The AR(1) trace is visibly smoother on the few-sample scale. \textbf {Right}: the corresponding empirical ACFs, alongside the real-data residual ACF (black). The v2 noise matches the real ACF; the v1 noise does not.}}{47}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces The three v2 templates --- the only shapes that have an empirical counterpart in the hand-labelled events of \cref {sec:reanalysis-labels}.}}{48}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces Five stochastic realisations of each v2 template, drawn with log-uniform amplitudes in $[15, 40]$ counts and log-uniform durations in $[30, 1000]$ samples.}}{49}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces First $100\,000$ samples (of $10^{7}$) of the v2 synthetic dataset. Anomaly spans are coloured by class. Note the sparse injection compared to \cref {fig:v1-synth-preview}: $\sim 15$ events in this view, against four in the v1 $30$k-sample preview.}}{50}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.5}{\ignorespaces v1 vs v2 synthetic data on the same window size. v1 has four visible bumps; v2 has zero. The marginal noise standard deviation is the same ($\sigma \approx 2.3$), but the local texture of v2 is smoother because of the AR(1) memory.}}{50}{figure.caption.35}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces v2 training dynamics. Left: train (dashed) and validation (solid) loss. Right: validation macro F1 (window level).}}{52}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces v2 event-level confusion matrices on the synthetic test segment. The leftmost column of each panel is the ``missed event'' column --- a true event that has no overlapping prediction. No event is missed by any of the three models, including the four shortest ones in the segment ($26$, $28$, $30$ and $45$ samples, near the lower bound of the v2 duration range). The 1D-CNN and CNN+Attention classify all $15$ events correctly; the ResNet has a single off-diagonal entry --- one $61$-sample \texttt {bell} predicted as \texttt {fast\_ascend}.}}{53}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces v2 cost--performance trade-off. \textbf {Left}: parameter count ($22.6$k, $74.5$k and $109.5$k for the 1D-CNN, ResNet and CNN+Attention respectively). \textbf {Centre}: total training time on a single RTX 5060 Laptop GPU (the 1D-CNN ran the full $35$ epochs in $\sim 3.7$ min, the CNN+Attention stopped early at epoch $21$ after $\sim 4.8$ min, the ResNet at epoch $26$ after $\sim 5.3$ min). \textbf {Right}: event-level macro F1 on the synthetic test segment. The 1D-CNN and the CNN+Attention reach a perfect $1.00$; the ResNet sits at $0.92$ because of the single misclassification discussed above.}}{55}{figure.caption.40}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.1}{\ignorespaces v2 robustness curves. \textbf {Left}: event-level macro F1 as a function of the background-noise multiplier. \textbf {Right}: macro F1 as a function of the anomaly-shape deformation multiplier. The dotted line marks the $50\%$ threshold.}}{57}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.2}{\ignorespaces Robustness curves of v1 (top row) and v2 (bottom row) on the same axes. The vertical position of the curves is not directly comparable between the two iterations --- v1 reports macro F1 over six classes, v2 over three --- but the qualitative behaviour is. The collapse of the 1D-CNN at high noise is much more brutal in v1 (drop to $\sim 7\%$) than in v2 (gentler drop to $\sim 4\%$ by the end of the sweep), and the residual / attention models are qualitatively similar in both iterations.}}{58}{figure.caption.42}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.1}{\ignorespaces v2 inference on the first $10\,000$ samples of February 2015. Blue: raw gamma dose (left axis). Grey: smoothed \texttt {Normal Background} confidence. Coloured lines: the three anomaly classes (right axis, $0$--$100\%$). Compared to \cref {fig:v1-real-inference}, the grey background-confidence curve is near $100\%$ throughout the quiet regions and only drops where the raw signal contains a visible bump.}}{61}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.2}{\ignorespaces ResNet 1D on the first $10\,000$ samples of February 2015. \textbf {Top}: v1, six classes, training on white-noise dataset --- sustained false-positive plateaus across the entire window. \textbf {Bottom}: v2, four classes, training on AR(1) dataset calibrated to the hand labels --- the model sits on \texttt {Normal} on the background and fires only at the labelled event around sample $3\,500$.}}{62}{figure.caption.44}%
